[gd_resource type="Resource" script_class="WeaponStats" load_steps=2 format=3]

[ext_resource type="Script" path="res://scripts/WeaponStats.gd" id="1"]

; The fishing rod (issue #273): a light launcher that fires a hook on a line.
; Lighter and faster than the grapple, with shorter range and less power.
;
; Flick the drag to fire: the hook flies out along the flick at
; 900 px/s for up to 800 px (#641). The rod moves what it hits to you (#629): a hook
; that meets terrain does not stick, it reels home empty. One that meets a
; player takes 6 off them (nothing off a teammate) and reels them in: while the
; drag is held a force of at most 3500 pulls them toward the holder, until the
; drag is released, they are within 60 px, either is KO'd, or 1.5 s pass. A
; head planted on the floor resists but still slides. The hook can fire again
; 0.25 s after it arrives home. The launcher itself swings as a light melee weapon: reach S, damage S (8),
; with a quick, light drive: faster than the grapple's (42 against 36) but weaker.
;
; The head is the launcher grip, a thin rod with a reel (#621) drawn as `art_outline`
; and filled by four circles. The hook sitting in it is `loaded_art`: drawn over the head
; while the hook is home, hidden while it is out, and colliding with nothing.
;
; The cast arcs (issue #625): the line used to be 400 px, a flight of about 0.44 s;
; #641 doubled it to 800 px so the cast stays out about twice as long (roughly 0.8 s,
; a little under double as gravity speeds the hook up). Gravity 900 px/s^2 sags the
; hook 0.5*900*0.44^2 = roughly 90 px by the old 400 px mark (about 22 px at 200 px
; out), so a platform at head height half way out is still hooked by a flick aimed
; a little high; the longer line sags further the further it runs.
;
; Force ceiling 6500, under the grapple's 7300.

[resource]
